+---
+id: dl-004-bulk-import-lenient-field-split-reuse-list-validation-flat-result
+type: decision-log
+title: "Bulk import: lenient field split, reuse list validation, flat result"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Teams want to paste a spreadsheet export into Tidy Tasks: one task per line, as
+`title;priority;tags`, priority optionally blank for "normal", tags space-separated, blank lines
+skipped. The request asks for a public helper, `importTasks(list, text)`, returning
+`{ imported, rejected }` with `rejected` as `{ line, reason }[]`, `line` counting from 1.
+
+This is bulk, line-oriented text parsing glued onto the existing `TaskList` API, not a new domain
+concept — `TaskList.add` already validates title, priority and tags (`dl-003`) and never throws
+(`r4-errors-as-result`). The only new decision is how to parse each line and how to report failures,
+not how to validate a task's content a second time.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Placed the helper in a new `src/import.ts` (not under `src/domain/`), exported from `src/index.ts`.
+  It only parses text and delegates to `list.add`; it carries no task-validation rules of its own, so
+  duplicating that logic was avoided.
+- Split each line on its first two semicolons only (`indexOf(';')` twice), not a 3-part
+  `split(';')`: a line with fewer than two semicolons is malformed (rejected before ever reaching
+  `list.add`), but everything after the second semicolon — even if it contains further semicolons —
+  is taken as the tags field, so the parser does not reject on a detail the spec never constrains.
+- Title and priority validity (empty title, over-length title, unknown priority, bad tag, duplicate
+  title) are *not* re-checked in `import.ts`: the parsed line is handed to `list.add` as-is, and a
+  rejection's `reason` is simply `result.error.message` from the `Result` it returns. This keeps
+  validation in one place (the list), and the rejection text is already the existing, user-facing
+  message.
+- An empty priority field (`title;;tags`) is parsed as `priority: undefined`, not `priority: ''`, so
+  `list.add` falls through to its own `normal` default rather than failing validation — "leave the
+  priority empty for a normal one" becomes "the field this helper doesn't pass along."
+- Lines are 1-indexed from `text.split(/\r\n|\n/)`'s array index, counting every line including blank
+  ones (blank lines are skipped — `imported` and `rejected` both ignore them — but still consume a
+  line number), so a rejection's `line` always matches the line the user would count in their pasted
+  text.
+- The return shape is the plain `{ imported, rejected }` the request specifies, not a `Result`:
+  `r4-errors-as-result` binds `src/domain/`, and this function cannot itself fail as a whole — every
+  line either adds a task or is recorded as a rejection, so there is no outer failure case to wrap.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- No new dependency was needed (`r1-no-new-dependency`); `import.ts` reads no clock and no randomness
+  (`r3-no-clock-or-randomness` — moot here since the restriction is scoped to `src/domain/`, but true
+  anyway); `importTasks`, `ImportRejection` and `ImportResult` all carry TSDoc (`r2-tsdoc-on-exports`).
+- Rejection reasons are exactly the list's own validation messages (e.g. `title must not be empty`),
+  so a wording change to those messages automatically flows through to bulk-import rejections with no
+  separate copy to maintain — and a line rejected for a reason specific to parsing (wrong shape) gets
+  a parser-authored message instead: `line must have the form "title;priority;tags"`.
+- `test/import.test.ts` covers: a title-only line, a line with priority and multiple tags, several
+  lines with blank lines interleaved (import counts, and line numbers after the blanks, both
+  unaffected), an invalid-priority line, an empty-title line, a shapeless line, and a duplicate-title
+  line — each a happy-path or an error-path per the testing directive, red-first since this behaviour
+  is new.
